          "question": "In general relativity, the Bianchi identities are the mathematical guarantee that energy and momentum are locally conserved.\n\n1. Beginning from the differential definition of the Riemann tensor, provide an alternative derivation of the Second Bianchi Identity:\n   $$\\nabla_m R^l_{ijk} + \\nabla_k R^l_{ijm} + \\nabla_j R^l_{ikm} = 0$$\n   using the Jacobi identity for commutators of covariant derivatives: $[[\\nabla_j, \\nabla_k], \\nabla_m] + [[\\nabla_k, \\nabla_m], \\nabla_j] + [[\\nabla_m, \\nabla_j], \\nabla_k] = 0$.\n2. Contract this identity twice to prove step-by-step:\n   $$\\nabla_i \\left( R^{ij} - \\frac{1}{2} R g^{ij} \\right) = 0$$\n3. Explain why adding a cosmological constant term $\\Lambda g^{ij}$ preserves the divergence-free condition:\n   $$\\nabla_i \\left( R^{ij} - \\frac{1}{2} R g^{ij} + \\Lambda g^{ij} \\right) = 0$$",
          "statement": "In general relativity, the Bianchi identities are the mathematical guarantee that energy and momentum are locally conserved.\n\n1. Beginning from the differential definition of the Riemann tensor, provide an alternative derivation of the Second Bianchi Identity:\n   $$\\nabla_m R^l_{ijk} + \\nabla_k R^l_{ijm} + \\nabla_j R^l_{ikm} = 0$$\n   using the Jacobi identity for commutators of covariant derivatives: $[[\\nabla_j, \\nabla_k], \\nabla_m] + [[\\nabla_k, \\nabla_m], \\nabla_j] + [[\\nabla_m, \\nabla_j], \\nabla_k] = 0$.\n2. Contract this identity twice to prove step-by-step:\n   $$\\nabla_i \\left( R^{ij} - \\frac{1}{2} R g^{ij} \\right) = 0$$\n3. Explain why adding a cosmological constant term $\\Lambda g^{ij}$ preserves the divergence-free condition:\n   $$\\nabla_i \\left( R^{ij} - \\frac{1}{2} R g^{ij} + \\Lambda g^{ij} \\right) = 0$$",
