          "solution": "### 1. Jacobi Identity Derivation\nFor any linear operators $A, B, C$, the Jacobi identity holds identically:\n$$[A, [B, C]] + [B, [C, A]] + [C, [A, B]] = 0$$\nLet $A = \\nabla_j, B = \\nabla_k, C = \\nabla_m$ acting on a test vector field $V^l$:\n$$[\\nabla_j, [\\nabla_k, \\nabla_m]] V^l + [\\nabla_k, [\\nabla_m, \\nabla_j]] V^l + [\\nabla_m, [\\nabla_j, \\nabla_k]] V^l = 0$$\nRecall that $[\\nabla_k, \\nabla_m] V^l = R^l_{p km} V^p$.\nThen:\n$$\\nabla_j ([\\nabla_k, \\nabla_m] V^l) = \\nabla_j (R^l_{p km} V^p) = (\\nabla_j R^l_{p km}) V^p + R^l_{p km} \\nabla_j V^p$$\nAnd:\n$$[\\nabla_k, \\nabla_m](\\nabla_j V^l) = R^l_{p km} \\nabla_j V^p - R^p_{j km} \\nabla_p V^l$$\nSubtracting these two yields:\n$$[\\nabla_j, [\\nabla_k, \\nabla_m]] V^l = (\\nabla_j R^l_{p km}) V^p + R^p_{j km} \\nabla_p V^l$$\nSumming the three cyclic permutations over $(j, k, m)$:\nThe first group gives:\n$$\\left( \\nabla_j R^l_{p km} + \\nabla_k R^l_{p mj} + \\nabla_m R^l_{p jk} \\right) V^p$$\nThe second group gives:\n$$\\left( R^p_{j km} + R^p_{k mj} + R^p_{m jk} \\right) \\nabla_p V^l$$\nBy the first (algebraic) Bianchi identity, the second group vanishes identically:\n$$R^p_{j km} + R^p_{k mj} + R^p_{m jk} = 0$$\nSince $V^p$ is arbitrary, the first group must vanish:\n$$\\nabla_j R^l_{p km} + \\nabla_k R^l_{p mj} + \\nabla_m R^l_{p jk} = 0 \\qquad \\blacksquare$$\n\n---\n\n### 2. Step-by-Step Twice Contracted Bianchi Identity\nStart with:\n$$\\nabla_m R^l_{ijk} + \\nabla_k R^l_{ijm} + \\nabla_j R^l_{ikm} = 0$$\nContract $l$ with $k$ (set $k = l$):\n$$\\nabla_m R_{ij} + \\nabla_l R^l_{ijm} - \\nabla_j R_{im} = 0$$\nContract with $g^{ij}$:\n$$g^{ij} \\nabla_m R_{ij} + g^{ij} \\nabla_l R^l_{ijm} - g^{ij} \\nabla_j R_{im} = 0$$\nUsing Ricci's theorem ($\\nabla g = 0$):\n- $g^{ij} \\nabla_m R_{ij} = \\nabla_m (g^{ij} R_{ij}) = \\nabla_m R$.\n- $g^{ij} R^l_{ijm} = -g^{ij} R^l_{imj} = -R^l_m \\implies g^{ij} \\nabla_l R^l_{ijm} = -\\nabla_l R^l_m$.\n- $g^{ij} \\nabla_j R_{im} = \\nabla_j R^j_m = \\nabla_l R^l_m$.\nCombining:\n$$\\nabla_m R - 2 \\nabla_l R^l_m = 0 \\implies \\nabla_l R^l_m = \\frac{1}{2} \\nabla_m R$$\nRaise index $m$ with $g^{mj}$:\n$$\\nabla_l R^{lj} = \\frac{1}{2} g^{mj} \\nabla_m R = \\frac{1}{2} \\nabla^j R$$\nTherefore:\n$$\\nabla_l \\left( R^{lj} - \\frac{1}{2} R g^{lj} \\right) = 0 \\implies \\nabla_i G^{ij} = 0 \\qquad \\blacksquare$$\n\n---\n\n### 3. Cosmological Constant Term\nConsider the augmented tensor:\n$$T^{ij}_{\\text{geom}} = R^{ij} - \\frac{1}{2} R g^{ij} + \\Lambda g^{ij} = G^{ij} + \\Lambda g^{ij}$$\nwhere $\\Lambda$ is a constant.\nTaking the covariant divergence:\n$$\\nabla_i \\left( G^{ij} + \\Lambda g^{ij} \\right) = \\nabla_i G^{ij} + \\nabla_i (\\Lambda g^{ij}) = 0 + \\Lambda (\\nabla_i g^{ij}) + (\\nabla_i \\Lambda) g^{ij}$$\nSince $\\Lambda$ is a constant, $\\nabla_i \\Lambda = \\partial_i \\Lambda = 0$.\nBy Ricci's Theorem, $\\nabla_i g^{ij} = 0$.\nThus:\n$$\\nabla_i \\left( G^{ij} + \\Lambda g^{ij} \\right) = 0 + 0 + 0 = 0 \\qquad \\blacksquare$$\nThis proves that the cosmological constant $\\Lambda$ is the unique scalar modification that keeps Einstein's equations strictly divergence-free!"
